WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:08.200
Hi very excited to be here.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:11.700
So I do a lot of
exploratory data

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:16.170
analysis and a lot of that flow
is built around reading data

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:17.430
into memory.

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:19.560
The first step is
we import some data

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:23.040
before we can transform,
visualize, model it.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:27.150
And if you're in this room, it
might be in this databases track,

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:29.250
it might be because a
lot of the time that's

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:34.030
not where the data we need
is. In a lot of organizations,

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:37.260
1e work with data that's in a
database, MySQL, PostgreSQL,

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:41.070
snowflake, SQL Server and a
lot of the most exciting data

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:44.770
analyses don't start from
reading a CSV into memory.

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:47.313
They start by hitting
data that's in SQL.

00:00:49.665 --> 00:00:51.790
I have a strong belief that
working with a database

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:53.855
should be as easy for
your team as possible.

00:00:53.855 --> 00:00:56.230
The last couple of years,
there's been a lot of attention

00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:58.840
to how can we build
R packages that

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:03.020
make working with make work
within a company really smooth.

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:05.050
That really helped people
get to the data that

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:10.050
matters for their
team for the team and.

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:11.700
That first kind of
really hit me when

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:15.540
I was in my first data scientist
role at Stack Overflow.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:18.690
So when I first got in
there, I was the first R user

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:23.190
and I ended up building
an internal package called

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:28.170
sqlstackr that managed
kind of a connecting

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:30.750
and working with the
data in those databases

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:32.340
and every job I've
had since I've

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:34.800
built some similar
iteration onto that,

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:38.340
onto that same template
of creating a database

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:42.070
package that wraps a
database connection.

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:46.400
So now we've opened
sourced it as dbcooper.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:49.330
So dbcooper is an--
it started as an R

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:53.920
package named after the famous
bandit, the hijacker, DB Cooper.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:55.810
And I'm excited to share that,

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:57.310
while it started,
as an R package,

00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:59.780
Michael Chao has actually
ported it to Python.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:03.980
So now there's both a
dbcooper R and Python version.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:07.070
And what they do
is to make it one

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:12.170
step to create a database
specific packages.

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:14.750
So if you're at a
company, Widgets Inc,

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:17.960
you can use dbcooper to create
an internal package, like widgetr

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:22.820
or widgetpy to help
analysts fluidly access

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:24.166
the data in your databases.

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:29.177
Let's talk about the problem
that it's solving. Mostly today,

00:02:29.177 --> 00:02:31.260
I'm to be talking from the
perspective of the R

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:31.810
package.

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:35.490
But a lot of these same
problems and solutions

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:37.470
exist in the Python version.

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:41.075
So what's it like to work
with a database from R?

00:02:43.838 --> 00:02:45.380
What might you need
to do when you're

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:48.000
analyzing data in a database?

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.120
Some common tasks.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:52.470
You may need to list the tables
that are in the database.

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:56.290
You may want to transform
tables using dplyr.

00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:58.710
So dbplyr is a
really powerful package

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:00.720
that lets you work with
a remote table, kind

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:02.760
of as if it were
an in-memory table.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.880
And who here has used dbplyr?

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:08.490
Really it is a really
fun experience, isn't it?

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:10.710
We sometimes can't
just use dbplyr.

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:13.770
We want to run our
custom SQL queries.

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:16.530
So we want to construct
SQL select statements

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:18.330
ourselves and try running them.

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:20.370
And sometimes we need
to execute a command,

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:23.330
like creating a table
or dropping a table.

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:28.820
So I'll give an example
here of how we might

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:30.540
work with the SQL connection.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:34.220
So this is a database
called lahman.

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:36.860
It's based on the Lahman
baseball statistics data

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:39.560
set, a really phenomenal
collection of tables,

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:41.730
and it comes built
into dbplyr.

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:44.220
So we've got this
lahman db object

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:46.780
that's a SQL connection and.

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:52.630
Let's start with how might
we go about listing tables?

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:54.500
We'd use the dbListTables

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.010
function from DBI.

00:03:56.010 --> 00:04:01.060
It would get us a vector of
the tables in the database.

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:02.830
If we wanted to work
with a remote table

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:05.530
as if it were a dbplyr
table, we'd

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:07.850
use the tibble function tbl.

00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:10.810
On this database, we want to
get one of the tables out of it.

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:12.460
In the case of
the lahman data, we

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:17.670
might be interested in its
table on batting statistics.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:19.320
If I want to run
a SQL query, I'd

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:21.120
still use the tbl
function, but now I

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:24.790
could use the SQL function to
actually construct that query.

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:26.880
So here I might be
writing a query where

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:30.360
I'm counting the batting
table grouped by player ID

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:34.110
and getting back
some results from it.

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:35.730
And if I want to
execute a command,

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:37.790
I might use the
dbExecute function.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:46.890
So these four
approaches, they work

00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:50.260
they do what they're
supposed to do.

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:52.840
But something really
bothers me about them.

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:55.260
Something is just fundamentally
a little bit harder

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:58.440
to use than I would expect it
to be something is they're not

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:02.590
they're not designed how I would
want to work with this data.

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:05.520
And I really try and figure
out what is bothering me

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:08.260
about these functions.

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:10.620
And I'll say, I
think that let's say

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:12.990
if I load up this database,
what really is bugging

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:17.360
me is that lahman db object.

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:20.040
Like in our environment, we've
got this lahman db object.

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:22.560
It's a SQL connection object.

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:25.050
And I don't know what that is.

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:27.840
It's for me, that's the
wrong level of abstraction.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:30.300
I want to be working
with individual tables.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:33.500
I want to be directly
writing SQL queries

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:35.010
and immediately executing them.

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:37.130
I don't necessarily I
don't know how I'm going

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:38.580
to pass this connection around.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:41.250
It's a clumsy way to
engage with a database.

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:46.520
So that's why I see that
there's a missing puzzle

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:50.480
piece in between DBI for creating
a connection to a database

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:53.695
and dbplyr for transforming
and exploring those tables.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:58.720
That's where dbcooper comes in.

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:01.260
It turns a database into
an opinionated collection

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:04.440
of functions to let us
explore it really fluidly.

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:09.270
Kind of as if it were
data that were in memory.

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:09.770
DB

00:06:09.770 --> 00:06:10.530
Cooper takes--

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:12.450
You only need to do one
function call to set up

00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:12.950
dbcooper,

00:06:12.950 --> 00:06:15.410
it's dbc init.

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:17.150
You pass it the
connection object

00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:18.600
and you give it a prefix.

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:20.433
If you've worked with
a lot of R packages,

00:06:20.433 --> 00:06:24.590
you know how useful it can be to have a
shared prefix. dbc init stores

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:26.760
and manages the
connection globally.

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:29.870
It generates a prefixed
function for important tasks

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:32.600
you would do with that database,
and really importantly, it

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:36.590
generates autocomplete friendly
accessors for tables.

00:06:36.590 --> 00:06:38.120
So I'll show how
each of those tasks

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:40.860
would be achieved in dbcooper.

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:43.640
Once you've run
dbc init, instead

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:47.970
of doing DB list tables, I would
just do lahman underscore list.

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:50.870
So that prefix that I passed,
it turned into a prefix

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:51.810
for the function.

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:55.130
Lahman list would
return the list of tables

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:56.520
that are in the database.

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:08.740
So lahman, if I want to
access just one table,

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:12.713
I use lahman underscore
TBL so I send in lahman tbl,

00:07:12.713 --> 00:07:14.380
then it can pass at
the name of a table.

00:07:14.380 --> 00:07:16.380
Get that one back.

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:18.010
If you want to
run a query, I do

00:07:18.010 --> 00:07:22.960
lahman underscore query
and pass the SQL.

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:26.600
And if I wanted to execute, I'd
use lahman underscore execute.

00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:28.100
So one thing to
notice about this is

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:30.370
it'ss taking the connection
object entirely out

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:32.560
of the picture,
and it's focusing

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:34.960
on opinionated set of
tasks that someone might

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:38.970
want to do with the database.

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:42.540
My favorite feature about
dbcooper is autocomplete.

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:45.242
So instead of so, whenever
I've worked with a database,

00:07:45.242 --> 00:07:47.200
it can be hard to remember
what the tables are.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:49.710
And I get kind of tired of
constantly referring back

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:53.310
to the documentation or
even to a list function

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:54.330
to see what they are.

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:58.830
And I don't like I would maybe
run into typos, et cetera

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:00.690
and it just doesn't
feel anything

00:08:00.690 --> 00:08:02.940
like working with
data that's in memory.

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:08.280
So dbcooper creates
one function for each

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:10.270
of the tables in your database

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:12.450
so that you can access
them with autocomplete.

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:13.950
So it's a much more
fluid experience

00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:15.825
where I could say where
if I go into a table,

00:08:15.825 --> 00:08:17.283
I can just start
typing it and see,

00:08:17.283 --> 00:08:19.440
Oh yeah, here are the
tables that I can't remember

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:21.482
exactly what this table
in my database is called,

00:08:21.482 --> 00:08:23.525
but I use autocomplete
to help me out.

00:08:26.085 --> 00:08:27.460
One thing I like
about that is it

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:29.980
makes something
like a complicated

00:08:29.980 --> 00:08:33.690
join much more concise
and much easier to read.

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:36.610
For me, again, it
really means I don't

00:08:36.610 --> 00:08:39.760
have to remember that I'm
working with SQL tables.

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:42.610
Using dbplyr
with remote tables

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:45.540
can feel a lot like using
dplyr with local tables.

00:08:49.330 --> 00:08:51.920
So I've been showing
examples of the R function.

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:53.950
What Michael Chao
has set up is really

00:08:53.950 --> 00:08:58.330
phenomenal, which is dbcooper
py where each of the-- it

00:08:58.330 --> 00:09:03.370
has these same syntax for
creating list, table, query

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:05.730
and execute.

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:08.680
But it also takes advantage
of his siuba package.

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:13.380
Siuba is a Python
port of dplyr

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:17.220
that supports both local
pandas data frames and data

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:18.600
and databases.

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:23.280
So here, you can actually see
that we could use the lahman

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:26.770
data and say dot TBL
for the salaries,

00:09:26.770 --> 00:09:30.190
and then pipe to count,
but all within Python.

00:09:30.190 --> 00:09:32.460
So one thing I think
is great about siuba is

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:35.100
if you're really
familiar with dplyr

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:37.598
and you're just going
over to Python, siuba

00:09:37.598 --> 00:09:38.890
is absolutely the way to do it.

00:09:38.890 --> 00:09:43.736
And dbcooper in Python is a way
to use that in your databases.

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:49.610
I'm going to show an
example of exploring

00:09:49.610 --> 00:09:53.120
a database using dbcooper.
So it's the New York Times COVID

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:56.050
19 database.

00:09:56.050 --> 00:09:59.560
Google makes public a large
set of data of databases.

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:03.985
Lets see 239 databases
in Google BigQuery.

00:10:03.985 --> 00:10:05.860
So this is a phenomenal
resource because that

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:11.050
means there are 239 packages
you could write wrapping

00:10:11.050 --> 00:10:12.870
each of these data sources.

00:10:12.870 --> 00:10:16.540
I'm going to show one, which
is the New York Times COVID 19

00:10:16.540 --> 00:10:18.890
database.

00:10:18.890 --> 00:10:21.300
So we start by creating
the connection.

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:21.800
dbcooper

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:23.050
doesn't help with this.

00:10:23.050 --> 00:10:25.880
We create it ourselves
using DBI dbConnect,

00:10:25.880 --> 00:10:27.740
really whatever approach
we would generally

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:30.540
use to create a
database connection.

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:35.010
And then all we have to do
with dbcooper is dbc init,

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:37.225
and we'll say we'll
use the prefix covid

00:10:37.225 --> 00:10:39.350
so all of our functions
we're going to use to access

00:10:39.350 --> 00:10:41.195
this are going to start
with the word covid.

00:10:43.970 --> 00:10:46.460
So now that I have
done dbc init, I

00:10:46.460 --> 00:10:49.260
could use COVID list
if I want to see, OK,

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:54.010
there are four tables
in this database.

00:10:54.010 --> 00:10:55.690
And if I want to
access one of them,

00:10:55.690 --> 00:10:59.110
I could say, OK, COVID
underscore US states.

00:10:59.110 --> 00:11:01.780
Now I've got this
autocomplete friendly way

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:03.640
to access one of
the tables and we

00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:06.390
see data that looks
like it's a time series.

00:11:06.390 --> 00:11:09.840
It might separate across
each of the US states.

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:14.290
It's got it's got both a column
for confirmed cases and deaths.

00:11:14.290 --> 00:11:16.930
In my understanding, it gets
updated daily so we could build

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:20.470
something on top of this
that unlike a static data set

00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:23.070
is going to stay up to date.

00:11:23.070 --> 00:11:25.980
And now that I've got it,
I can work with this data

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:27.220
as if it were local.

00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:31.560
I can work with it and I
can filter, I can arrange,

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:33.700
I can do visualizations.

00:11:33.700 --> 00:11:36.140
So I can let's say we
pipe it through it.

00:11:36.140 --> 00:11:38.040
This is actually all
this aggregation,

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:39.730
this filter, this
arrange, this mutate

00:11:39.730 --> 00:11:42.210
was all turned into a SQL,

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:45.930
a SQL query, gets run,
gets brought into memory

00:11:45.930 --> 00:11:47.640
only when we plot it.

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:49.620
And this thing that I
really like about dbcooper

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:51.173
is I just forget I'm using it.

00:11:51.173 --> 00:11:53.340
As soon as you've got these
tables, you kind of just

00:11:53.340 --> 00:11:55.590
go right back into
your normal flow.

00:11:55.590 --> 00:11:57.990
I never liked when I
worked with SQL databases

00:11:57.990 --> 00:12:00.300
that I'd have to
take one step to do

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:01.840
the select and the collect.

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:04.890
And now I'm working in my
traditional exploratory data

00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:06.670
analysis flow. Here,

00:12:06.670 --> 00:12:09.270
you can basically you can barely
tell where the database ends

00:12:09.270 --> 00:12:12.260
and where your exploratory
data analysis begins.

00:12:16.030 --> 00:12:19.930
Where dbcooper really shines is in
creating an opinionated package

00:12:19.930 --> 00:12:22.550
around a database.

00:12:22.550 --> 00:12:23.700
So I'll give an example.

00:12:23.700 --> 00:12:26.270
As I said, I first
developed this kind of approach

00:12:26.270 --> 00:12:28.303
while I was working
at Stack Overflow.

00:12:28.303 --> 00:12:29.720
So it's appropriate
that we set up

00:12:29.720 --> 00:12:33.770
a package that wraps a public
Stack Overflow database, which

00:12:33.770 --> 00:12:37.940
is one of the BigQuery
public databases is Stack Overflow

00:12:37.940 --> 00:12:42.030
questions, answers, tags.

00:12:42.030 --> 00:12:46.530
So around this
database we set up

00:12:46.530 --> 00:12:51.210
the stack BigQuery
package. This is a package

00:12:51.210 --> 00:12:53.860
I put up just as an example of
package that could be created.

00:12:53.860 --> 00:12:57.480
We pick the database, now
let's wrap it with a package.

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:01.180
And the way that we'd go about
doing that is three steps.

00:13:01.180 --> 00:13:04.120
First, we write database
specific connection code.

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:06.900
So anything in terms of setting
up credentials, etc., that's

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:08.990
going to live
inside this package.

00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:19.800
Then critically in the
dot, the dot on load and on

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:21.340
unload functions.

00:13:21.340 --> 00:13:25.440
Those are functions that run
every time a package is loaded

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:27.810
or unloaded, we're
going to create

00:13:27.810 --> 00:13:29.790
one of those
database connections

00:13:29.790 --> 00:13:32.320
and then use dbc init.

00:13:32.320 --> 00:13:35.850
So this means that as soon as
somebody loads in this package,

00:13:35.850 --> 00:13:38.130
it's going to
populate the package's

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:42.133
namespace with functions that
start with that prefix stack.

00:13:45.340 --> 00:13:47.510
Now every database is
different and the ways

00:13:47.510 --> 00:13:49.870
that we explore them
are different, too.

00:13:49.870 --> 00:13:52.920
So the next step

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:55.810
would be adding
database-specific verbs.

00:13:55.810 --> 00:13:58.200
Something that I love
to do with Stack Overflow data

00:13:58.200 --> 00:14:02.550
is look at, say, patterns
of trends of tags over time.

00:14:02.550 --> 00:14:04.440
I'm really interested
in can I aggregate

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:07.350
at the tag level that
requires joining together

00:14:07.350 --> 00:14:09.360
a couple tables in
the database and doing

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:11.110
a little bit of custom logic.

00:14:11.110 --> 00:14:14.640
That kind of custom database
specific logic or problem

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:16.830
specific logic or
company-specific logic

00:14:16.830 --> 00:14:20.220
is really a good fit for a
package that uses dbcooper

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:23.880
because it's going to mean that
the person using this package

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:26.295
actually does this analysis,

00:14:26.295 --> 00:14:28.420
it's going to everything
should be set up for them.

00:14:28.420 --> 00:14:29.045
So when you do

00:14:29.045 --> 00:14:31.240
library stackbigquery,

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:33.660
now we've got a function
stack underscore

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:37.710
for any of the we've got stack
list, stack table, stack,

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:41.970
execute, stack query and one
of these functions for each

00:14:41.970 --> 00:14:43.300
of the table accessors.

00:14:43.300 --> 00:14:45.180
So basically by
loading library, it's

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:47.742
as if every table
just got loaded right

00:14:47.742 --> 00:14:48.700
into their environment.

00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:51.190
So they can use it.

00:14:51.190 --> 00:14:53.460
Here's an example of an
analysis that I would do, but.

00:14:57.680 --> 00:14:58.652
So here we go.

00:14:58.652 --> 00:15:00.110
So here's an example
of an analysis

00:15:00.110 --> 00:15:03.135
that I would do, for
instance, of tags over time.

00:15:03.135 --> 00:15:04.760
So this is an
aggregation that compares

00:15:04.760 --> 00:15:08.810
a couple of technologies like
Python, JavaScript, Java and R

00:15:08.810 --> 00:15:13.190
in terms of the percentage of
Stack Overflow questions each

00:15:13.190 --> 00:15:17.270
was done by month by
month that are tagged

00:15:17.270 --> 00:15:19.340
with this particular language.

00:15:19.340 --> 00:15:24.200
So we can notice there's a
use of that summarized tags

00:15:24.200 --> 00:15:26.030
function that's
database specific

00:15:26.030 --> 00:15:29.640
and then there's just some db,
some dplyr, some ggplot2.

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:31.580
And again really
quickly, you can

00:15:31.580 --> 00:15:33.955
forget that you're working
with a database at all.

00:15:37.870 --> 00:15:40.540
So we learn from the dbcooper
package is kind of really

00:15:40.540 --> 00:15:41.980
two takeaways.

00:15:41.980 --> 00:15:44.590
One is don't underestimate
the value of small usability

00:15:44.590 --> 00:15:48.063
improvements that
affect you every day.

00:15:48.063 --> 00:15:49.480
And second is that
a good database

00:15:49.480 --> 00:15:51.996
package lets you forget
about the package entirely.

00:15:56.770 --> 00:15:59.950
See, once we've got
this data loaded,

00:15:59.950 --> 00:16:02.770
it's really as if we just
had instead of looking

00:16:02.770 --> 00:16:04.475
at that one connection object.

00:16:04.475 --> 00:16:06.850
And now we're always working
with that connection object.

00:16:06.850 --> 00:16:11.670
Now we're just working with
all the tables in the database.

00:16:11.670 --> 00:16:14.880
So next time you're working
with a database connection,

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:17.020
it just takes one line.

00:16:17.020 --> 00:16:17.520
Try

00:16:17.610 --> 00:16:18.130
dbcooper.

00:16:18.130 --> 00:16:19.570
I'd love to hear what you think.

00:16:19.570 --> 00:16:21.440
Thank you.

